description:
FOUND by s540c (2026-11-13) covering the merge review's `fork after a dropped handle' probe for #2006 PART 2.

perl 5.40.3 (probe ~/pcl/.claude/worktrees/agent-ab2915f40902e154e/scratch/s540c/r541/r4b.pl):
  use Fcntl ':flock';
  sub lk { open my $fh, "<", $f or die; flock($fh, LOCK_EX) or die; return $fh }
  sub ldrop { my $fh = lk(); return 1 }
  ldrop();
  my $pid = fork();
  if ($pid == 0) { open my $c, ">>", $f or die; flock($c, LOCK_EX) or die; print "child locked\n"; exit 0 }
  waitpid($pid, 0); print "parent: child status $?\n";
perl:          child locked / parent: child status 0
s540c tree:    hangs (timeout 30 s): the child's blocking flock runs the collector -- in the CHILD, which closes
               only the child's copy of the descriptor; a flock(2) lock belongs to the open file DESCRIPTION, which
               the parent's uncollected copy keeps; the parent sits in waitpid.
main 24988e35: hangs the same way (and every later lock of the file from the parent too).
Same cause, NB form (r4.pl row D): the child's `flock(LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB)' answers false where perl answers true.

Not built in s540c, because the only fix is a collection BEFORE fork, and its cost lands on every fork of a
program that locks: measured on the s540c tree, one trigger collection costs 7.9 ms with a 50,000-hash live heap
(r541/cost.pl; perl's flock loop 0.02 ms an iteration), and a program holding a LIVE lock (a pidfile) and forking
per request would pay it at every fork.  Options for the reviewing session:
 (a) collect before `fork' / a forking pipe open when *p-flock-files* is non-empty (cheap test; every fork of a
     lock-using program pays one full collection);
 (b) track the streams that hold a lock now (a weak set kept by %p-flock-note / LOCK_UN / close) and collect only
     when one of them is no longer referenced -- which cannot be known without the collection itself, so (b) costs
     the same as (a) for a program that keeps a lock;
 (c) leave it: no CPAN board file forks after a dropped lock (Path-Tiny's tests do not fork).
